    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pick\n\n### Description\n\nI use osc52 explicitly everywhere, this is needed because I work on a Windows machine, on Windows Terminal, ssh'd into a linux VM, running tmux, running neovim inside. I paste with Ctrl + shift +V out of habit. I then explicitly have osc52 overrides to pass everything back up through the stack (neovim -> tmux -> ssh -> windows terminal). I do this because no matter what DE I'm using (and even tty, though I'd use tmux as my \"DE\" instead) I have access to a clipboard that works across all applications I care about.  \n\nAs far as I know, there's no way to programmatically have neovim paste your system's outer clipboard (no matter how tunneled) into its application. Not sure if it's technically possible full stop, and furthermore that's a huge security risk. So the paste must come from the outer DE app (say, Windows Terminal itself) pasting stuff into the terminal.\n\nThis means that solutions such as https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/2258 will not work. Concretely: I cannot copy paste a string in firefox on my Windows host and then paste it into the finder anymore. \n\nSo the only way is to intercept the hook. I tried hijacking the plugin by retaining the original `vim.paste` and setting it towards the end - I see the issue is that it pastes inline with the query results. Fine, I see the technical issues with allowing system paste, but surely this is fixable, somehow? \n\nI'm also happy to adopt alternative workflows. I suspect the codes in 2258 are fixable with deep hacks by looking into how `vim.paste` is implemented to also work cleanly with my workflow but I'm not a neovim expert unfortunately ",
